Participants: Browser and Server

1. Submit the form.

2. The browser sends an HTTP POST request to the server endpoint /new_note.

3. The server responds with the HTTP status code 302 Found.

4. This response indicates a URL redirection. The server instructs the browser to make a new HTTP GET request to the URL specified in the Location header, which points to /notes.

5. The browser reloads the Notes page. This reload triggers three additional HTTP requests to retrieve the following resources:
- main.css — the stylesheet.
- main.js — the JavaScript code.
- data.json — the raw note data.
Form Submission and Server-Side Processing
6. The HTML <form> element uses the action and method attributes to define where and how the form data is submitted. In this case, the form sends an HTTP POST request to /new_note.

7. The form data is sent in the body of the POST request.

8. The server accesses the submitted data through the req.body property of the request object (req).

9. The server creates a new note object and adds it to an array called notes.

10. Each note object contains two fields:
- content: stores the actual content of the note.
- date: stores the date and time when the note was created.
11. The server does not store new notes in a database. Therefore, all notes added during runtime are lost when the server restarts.

